1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:02,450
July 28, 1402.

2
00:00:02,500 --> 00:00:09,750
On the plain of Ankara, two great Turkish
armies stood face to face.

3
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:16,620
One sultan would end the day in chains,
betrayed by his own men.

4
00:00:16,670 --> 00:00:19,950
But why would they turn on him?

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:25,110
Two Turkish rulers, two empires, and a
world too small for both of them.

6
00:00:25,160 --> 00:00:30,570
In the East, Timur, the lame conqueror who
left towers of skulls behind him.

7
00:00:30,620 --> 00:00:36,950
In the West, Bayezid, the Thunderbolt, the sultan
who crushed the Crusaders at Nicopolis.

8
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:43,730
In the year 1400, two fugitive kings fled
from Timur's armies

9
00:00:43,780 --> 00:00:48,060
and found shelter at the Ottoman court.

10
00:00:48,110 --> 00:00:53,950
They were Sultan Ahmed Jalayir, the ruler
of Baghdad,

11
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:59,090
and Kara Yusuf, the chief of the Black
Sheep Turkomans.

12
00:00:59,140 --> 00:01:01,800
And so it began with letters.

13
00:01:01,850 --> 00:01:06,560
Timur wrote: Hand over the princes who
fled to you.

14
00:01:06,610 --> 00:01:10,950
And remember who you are, the son of a
boatman.

15
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:15,950
Bayezid answered: I do not betray those
who seek my protection.

16
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:17,150
Come, lame one.

17
00:01:17,200 --> 00:01:22,870
And if you do not come, may your wives be
divorced from you three times.

18
00:01:22,920 --> 00:01:27,950
In the Islamic world, a threefold divorce
could never be undone.

19
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:33,550
To wish it upon another man was to call
him less than a man,

20
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:37,090
unable to keep even his own household.

21
00:01:37,140 --> 00:01:42,870
It was among the gravest insults a ruler
could put in writing,

22
00:01:42,920 --> 00:01:45,950
and Timur would never forgive it.

23
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:48,770
Those words could not be taken back.

24
00:01:48,820 --> 00:01:51,980
That summer, Timur marched into Anatolia.

25
00:01:52,030 --> 00:01:55,430
He took Sivas, and Bayezid's son died
there.

26
00:01:55,480 --> 00:01:59,740
But Timur did not stay. He turned south to
burn Aleppo,

27
00:01:59,790 --> 00:02:02,950
and Bayezid believed the storm had passed

28
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:06,940
and returned to the walls of
Constantinople. He was wrong.

29
00:02:06,990 --> 00:02:09,620
In the spring of 1402, Timur came back.

30
00:02:09,670 --> 00:02:14,170
Bayezid lifted the siege and marched east
through the summer heat,

31
00:02:14,220 --> 00:02:17,950
but Timur slipped around him and was
waiting at Ankara.

32
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,150
There, under the July sun, Timur cut off
the river.

33
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:24,230
North of the battlefield, near the town of
Çubuk,

34
00:02:24,280 --> 00:02:29,950
Timur's men dammed the stream and turned its water
into a reservoir behind their own lines.

35
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:34,620
Downstream, where the Ottomans would make
their camp, the riverbed ran dry,

36
00:02:34,670 --> 00:02:36,860
and the Ottoman army fought thirsty.

37
00:02:36,910 --> 00:02:40,970
Bayezid's Serbian knights proved their
loyalty to the Thunderbolt,

38
00:02:41,020 --> 00:02:45,950
and no one resisted Timur harder than they
did. His janissaries held the center.

39
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:52,090
Then, in the heart of the battle, the
Tatar horsemen turned and rode to Timur.

40
00:02:52,140 --> 00:02:56,420
Why? Timur had sent them secret word
before the battle.

41
00:02:56,470 --> 00:03:02,950
We are of one blood, of one steppe. Why die for
a sultan who keeps you as subjects?

42
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:08,640
And Bayezid, it was said, had kept his treasury
locked while his soldiers went unpaid.

43
00:03:08,690 --> 00:03:13,790
The Anatolian soldiers, seeing their old
lords at Timur's side, followed them.

44
00:03:13,840 --> 00:03:18,950
Bayezid fought on, on a hill with his last
loyal men, until the sun went down.

45
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:24,050
By nightfall, the Thunderbolt was a
prisoner.

46
00:03:24,100 --> 00:03:34,140
Some chronicles whisper that Timur carried him from
city to city, locked in an iron cage.

47
00:03:34,190 --> 00:03:35,950
Truth or legend?

48
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:41,530
No one knows for certain. Eight months
later, he was dead,

49
00:03:41,580 --> 00:03:45,760
and the Ottoman Empire nearly died with
him.

50
00:03:45,810 --> 00:03:49,600
His sons fought each other for 11 years.

51
00:03:49,650 --> 00:03:55,950
Timur died in 1405 on his way to China,
but the Ottomans survived.

52
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:02,850
And 51 years later, Bayezid's great-
grandson took Constantinople.
